  "reason": "Evidence: RNA (PCA n2000) is the dominant modality (0.485 acc) while image alone is very weak (0.208 acc). The multimodal gain (+4.2%) is modest and may be limited by the quality of the RNA representation. PCA is a linear method; whether a learned encoder (scvi_n200) captures nonlinear gene-expression structure that PCA misses is the single most important unresolved question, because it affects every downstream configuration (RNA-only, multimodal, multiprofile). Question: Does replacing PCA with a learned scvi encoder improve classification in the simplest RNA-only, single-profile setting? This is a clean single-variable change from config_001 (only the RNA encoder differs). Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It isolates the RNA-encoder effect without confounding from fusion, aggregation, or modality changes. (2) The result directly gates many future experiments—if scvi substantially outperforms PCA, we pivot to scvi-based multimodal configs; if it does not, PCA is sufficient and we redirect budget toward multiprofile/aggregation or fusion strategy. (3) Under a limited budget, resolving this fundamental representation question before adding complexity (multiprofile, weighted fusion, ViT-L) avoids wasting experiments on configurations built on a suboptimal encoder assumption."
}

